================================================================================================================
ADVERSE SELECTION
================================================================================================================
  entry signals            : 2,366
  fully filled             : 99 (4.2%)
  partially filled         : 1,630 (68.9%)
  gross edge, ALL signals  : $    2.92 / trade  (n=2,366)
  gross edge, FILLED only  : $    5.44 / trade  (n=99)
  adverse selection        : $    2.52 / trade (+86.2%)

  Filled orders are BETTER than the signal average. That is FAVOURABLE
  selection, the opposite of what a maker should expect, and it is the
  single most suspicious thing this script can print. It means the fill
  filter is not merely thinning the sample -- it is choosing a better
  one. The control rows are what decide whether that belongs to the
  model or to the filter:
    maker_taker  (model direction) : $+2.98/trade over 99 trades
    mk_tk[random] (coin flip)      : $-0.97/trade over 108 trades
    The coin flip loses money through the same filter, so the model
    row is not just the filter. Report the GAP between the two as
    the model's contribution, not the model row on its own.

================================================================================================================
THE MODEL'S CONTRIBUTION  (model minus control, same execution, same fill rule)
================================================================================================================
  Not the model row. The GAP. The model row includes whatever the entry
  gate and the fill filter contribute on their own, and those need no
  forecast; only the difference from a control that uses the same gate and
  the same filter is attributable to the STGAT.

  vs coin flip                +3.95 $/trade   95% CI [+1.99, +5.93]   excludes 0 -- a real contribution
  vs no-model reversion       +5.75 $/trade   95% CI [+4.12, +7.59]   excludes 0 -- a real contribution

  The two rows are NOT the same kind of comparison:
    vs coin flip  -- identical entry gate, identical fill filter, only the
                     direction differs. This is the causal control, and it
                     is the one that isolates the forecast.
    vs reversion  -- a different strategy with its own entry gate and its
                     own trade set. Losing to it says the model picks more
                     EXPENSIVE trades, not that its signal is worse; check
                     gross/trade against cost/trade before concluding.

  Resampled over baskets drawn once per replicate and applied to BOTH
  books, so the two means move together and the difference is a real
  interval rather than two independent noise draws subtracted.

================================================================================================================
WHAT THE FILL FILTER SELECTED
================================================================================================================
  Requiring flow to oppose the position on EVERY leg is a strong condition.
  If the surviving baskets differ systematically from the rest, the maker
  result is about that subpopulation, not about maker execution.

  population          n        legs/basket   mean leg price c  mean |delta_pred|  mean |delta_real|
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  all             2,366               5.04              22.95             0.0327             0.0458
  filled             99               3.66              32.07             0.0349             0.0635
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Divergence in 'legs/basket' or 'mean leg price' is a composition shift:
  fees scale with both, so a filter that prefers cheaper or smaller baskets
  lowers costs for reasons that have nothing to do with the forecast.

================================================================================================================
HOW TO READ THIS
================================================================================================================
  Quote maker_taker. Entry is patient -- an unfilled post costs nothing --
  but the exit is not optional, so crossing it is the honest assumption.
  maker_maker assumes you can also leave whenever you like, which is the
  assumption that makes every maker backtest look good.

  Compare against taker_taker in the SAME table rather than against the
  earlier run: same events, same fill model, one difference.

  If maker_taker is positive, check three things before believing it:
    1. the fill rate -- a 5% fill rate on 2,366 signals is ~120 trades, and
       a positive mean over 120 trades needs its CI, not its point estimate;
    2. that --fill-model optimistic gives a MATERIALLY better number, which
       is what proves the fill rule was actually binding;
    3. that the win rate did not collapse relative to the taker row -- if it
       did, you are being filled on the wrong side and the profit is coming
       from the spread alone, which a real counterparty would not hand over.
